<Doc title="I2C" subtitle="five devices, two wires" category="Buses" id="i2c">

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>line</Cell><Cell>Pico</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">SDA</Cell><Cell class="serial">GP4</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">SCL</Cell><Cell class="serial">GP5</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="power">pull-ups</Cell><Cell class="power">~4.7k to 3V3, both lines</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>device</Cell><Cell>address</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>display</Cell><Cell>0x3C</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>ToF</Cell><Cell>0x29</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>IMU</Cell><Cell>0x68</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Draw width="660" height="250">
# I2C on GP4/GP5: two wires the length of the page, three devices on both of
# them, and 4.7k pull-ups to 3V3 at the controller end.
#
# SDA is warm and SCL is cool, and they keep those two colours everywhere -
# on the trunk, on the drops, and on the junction dots.

# ---- the controller
setColor black
fillRect 30 66 118 178
setColor grey
setWidth 1
drawRect 30 66 118 178
setColor white
setTextSize 11
drawText 50 74 "Pico 2 W"
setColor grey
setTextSize 10
drawText 46 89 "controller"

# The two pads, straddling the board edge the way a real header does.
setColor brCyan
fillRect 110 103 126 117
fillRect 110 127 126 141
setColor brWhite
setTextSize 11
drawText 86 104 "GP4"
drawText 86 128 "GP5"

# ---- the two wires, the length of the page
setColor brYellow
setWidth 2
drawLine 126 110 610 110
setTextSize 11
drawText 614 104 "SDA"
setColor brBlue
drawLine 126 134 610 134
drawText 614 128 "SCL"
setColor grey
setTextSize 10
drawText 140 91 "data"
drawText 140 143 "clock"

# ---- pull-ups to 3V3
setColor power
setWidth 2
drawLine 176 36 236 36
setTextSize 11
drawText 240 31 "3V3"

setColor grey
setWidth 1
drawLine 176 36 176 54
drawLine 176 84 176 110
setColor yellow
drawRect 170 54 182 84
setColor brYellow
fillCircle 176 110 3

setColor grey
drawLine 206 36 206 54
# This drop crosses SDA at y=110 on its way to SCL and does NOT join it.
drawLine 206 84 206 134
setColor yellow
drawRect 200 54 212 84
setColor brBlue
fillCircle 206 134 3

setColor white
setTextSize 10
drawText 165 17 "pull-ups, ~4.7k"
setColor grey
drawText 340 17 "a dot is a join - wires that merely cross do not connect"

# ---- the devices, all three on the same pair
# display 0x3C
setColor black
fillRect 266 174 334 226
setColor grid
drawRect 266 174 334 226
setColor brWhite
setTextSize 11
drawText 279 186 "display"
setColor green
drawText 288 204 "0x3C"
setColor brYellow
setWidth 1
drawLine 288 110 288 174
fillCircle 288 110 3
setColor brBlue
drawLine 312 134 312 174
fillCircle 312 134 3

# ToF 0x29
setColor black
fillRect 366 174 434 226
setColor grid
drawRect 366 174 434 226
setColor brWhite
setTextSize 11
drawText 391 186 "ToF"
setColor green
drawText 388 204 "0x29"
setColor brYellow
drawLine 388 110 388 174
fillCircle 388 110 3
setColor brBlue
drawLine 412 134 412 174
fillCircle 412 134 3

# IMU 0x68
setColor black
fillRect 466 174 534 226
setColor grid
drawRect 466 174 534 226
setColor brWhite
setTextSize 11
drawText 491 186 "IMU"
setColor green
drawText 488 204 "0x68"
setColor brYellow
drawLine 488 110 488 174
fillCircle 488 110 3
setColor brBlue
drawLine 512 134 512 174
fillCircle 512 134 3
</Draw>

<Note><Bold>Write a scanner first.</Bold> Walk <Code>0x08</Code> to
<Code>0x77</Code>, ping each address, print the ones that answer. It needs no
driver for any particular chip and turns "is my wiring right" into a yes or
no.</Note>

<Warn><Bold>Two devices at the same address cannot share a bus.</Bold> The four
ToF sensors all ship at <Code>0x29</Code>, so XSHUT on
<Code>GP10</Code>-<Code>GP13</Code> holds three in reset while the fourth is
re-addressed.</Warn>

</Doc>
